# Test assertions that read a source file's TEXT (via readFileSync or Vite's
# `?raw`) and match a line-anchored pattern (`…$`, `.split('\n')`, a multi-line
# `toContain`) fail on a Windows checkout and only there: `core.autocrlf=true`
# (the Git-for-Windows default) rewrites these to CRLF in the working tree, so a
# `\r` sits before every line end and the pattern cannot match — green on CI's
# Linux runner, red for every Windows contributor. Pinning the working-tree
# ending to LF fixes the whole class at checkout, for every current and future
# such test, rather than each test normalizing its own read.
#
# eol=lf (not just `text`) forces LF regardless of the user's core.autocrlf. The
# blobs are already LF in the index, so this only affects the working-tree
# checkout — it introduces no content change. Mirrors the root .gitattributes,
# which pins the same ending for *.sh and the data files shell scripts parse.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf

# public/vendor/lucide-react.mjs is compared byte for byte against the string
# scripts/gen-vendor-lucide.mjs builds (src/test/vendorLucideStub.test.ts, and
# the generator's own --check), so it belongs to the class above even though it
# is .mjs. Without this pin a CRLF checkout reports the unchanged stub as STALE.
# Only this file: the other .mjs here are read line-wise or not at all, and
# pinning all 860 of them would be a working-tree change far beyond the class.
public/vendor/lucide-react.mjs text eol=lf
